**Read:** [Petrology — Stephen A. Nelson, Tulane EENS 2120](../sources/Petrology-Nelson-Tulane.lingua#r_1MNEKH4WW2GQRD4FZWNRD1GDPZ), the complete fractional-crystallization/trace-element notes; [Petrology — Timothy Grove, MIT 12.109](../sources/MIT-Petrology-12-109.lingua#r_6Q1GZJKSZXQBM4N19CTRYG64TG), the full differentiation lectures and assignments; [Advanced Igneous Petrology — Timothy Grove, MIT 12.490](../sources/MIT-Advanced-Igneous-Petrology.lingua#r_3R0PJP97ZPE1RSGPPGY8WXXQZF), the complete relevant natural/experimental case.

Crystallization changes phase assemblages; fractional crystallization additionally removes phases from continued exchange with the evolving liquid under a model. Crystal settling, compaction, filtering, flow, and other processes may separate material, but their effectiveness needs physical and textural evidence.

For a trace constituent with constant bulk D and continuously removed equilibrium crystals, the ideal Rayleigh liquid relation is C_l/C₀ = F^(D−1), where F is the remaining liquid mass fraction. With invented D = 0.1 and F = 0.2, the enrichment is about 4.257. This differs from batch melting because the history and mass account differ.

An evolving crystallizing assemblage can change D, and an actual rock may be a cumulate, mixed liquid/crystal sample, altered body, or disequilibrium population. A straight trend on a transformed plot is not unique evidence that one constant-D Rayleigh history occurred.

**Work:** derive the differential mass balance and integrate it under stated assumptions. Check endmember/limiting cases and compare liquid with accumulated removed-solid composition, closing the original element account. Reconstruct a published differentiation argument with modal, mineral, and textural evidence, then test an accumulation/mixing alternative.

Read the full fractional-crystallization treatment and original natural/experimental papers in the assigned advanced case. The next mixing and storage studies add processes whose signatures can overlap with this convenient model.

